Let $A$ be a real $2\times2$ matrix with trace $\tau$ and determinant $\Delta$; its eigenvalues are $\lambda_\pm=\tfrac12\big(\tau\pm\sqrt{\tau^2-4\Delta}\big)$. The phase portrait of $\dot x=Ax$ is, up to a linear change of coordinates, determined by $(\tau,\Delta)$ and (on the parabola) the diagonalizability of $A$:
- $\Delta<0$: a **saddle** (real eigenvalues of opposite signs);
- $\Delta>0$, $\tau^2>4\Delta$: a **node**, stable if $\tau<0$, unstable if $\tau>0$;
- $\Delta>0$, $\tau^2<4\Delta$: a **spiral**, stable if $\tau<0$, unstable if $\tau>0$; if $\tau=0$ a **centre** (closed ellipses);
- $\tau^2=4\Delta>0$: a **degenerate node** (one eigendirection) or a **star** ($A=\lambda I$);
- $\Delta=0$: a line of equilibria (or all of the plane, if $A=0$).

A truth assignment $v$ **satisfies** $\varphi$ if $\bar v(\varphi)=T$. A set $\Sigma$ of formulas **tautologically implies** $\tau$, written $\Sigma \vDash \tau$, if every truth assignment (for the letters involved) that satisfies every member of $\Sigma$ also satisfies $\tau$. In particular:
- $\tau$ is a **tautology** ($\vDash\tau$) if every assignment satisfies it;
- $\varphi$ is **satisfiable** if some assignment satisfies it, and **unsatisfiable** otherwise;
- $\Sigma \vDash \tau$ if and only if $\Sigma \cup \{\neg\tau\}$ is unsatisfiable.

For finite $\Sigma$ all of these can be decided with a truth table. An assignment that satisfies $\Sigma$ but not $\tau$ is a **countermodel**.

The basic convex sets of $\R^n$ are
(a) a **hyperplane** $\{x:a^\top x=b\}$ and the **halfspace** $\{x:a^\top x\le b\}$, $a\ne0$;
(b) a **Euclidean ball** $B(x_c,r)=\{x:\norm{x-x_c}_2\le r\}$ — indeed every norm ball $\{x:\norm{x-x_c}\le r\}$;
(c) an **ellipsoid** $\{x:(x-x_c)^\top P^{-1}(x-x_c)\le1\}$ with $P\in\mathbb S^n_{++}$ (symmetric positive definite), whose semi-axes are $\sqrt{\lambda_i(P)}$ along the eigenvectors of $P$;
(d) a **polyhedron** $\{x:Ax\preceq b,\ Cx=d\}$, a finite intersection of halfspaces and hyperplanes, and a **polytope** if it is bounded.
All of them are convex.

Let $\mathfrak A$ be a structure and $s$ an assignment of elements of $|\mathfrak A|$ to the variables. Extend $s$ to terms by $\bar s(x) = s(x)$, $\bar s(c) = c^{\mathfrak A}$ and $\bar s(f(t_1,\dots,t_n)) = f^{\mathfrak A}(\bar s(t_1),\dots,\bar s(t_n))$. Then **$\mathfrak A$ satisfies $\varphi$ with $s$**, written $\mathfrak A \models \varphi[s]$, is defined by recursion on $\varphi$:
- $\mathfrak A \models (t_1 = t_2)[s]$ iff $\bar s(t_1) = \bar s(t_2)$, and $\mathfrak A \models R(t_1,\dots,t_n)[s]$ iff $(\bar s(t_1),\dots,\bar s(t_n)) \in R^{\mathfrak A}$;
- $\neg$ and the binary connectives follow their truth tables;
- $\mathfrak A \models \forall x\,\varphi[s]$ iff $\mathfrak A \models \varphi[s(x|a)]$ for **every** $a \in |\mathfrak A|$, and $\mathfrak A \models \exists x\,\varphi[s]$ iff this holds for **some** $a$.

Here $s(x|a)$ agrees with $s$ except that it sends $x$ to $a$. Whether $\mathfrak A\models\varphi[s]$ depends only on $s$ restricted to the free variables of $\varphi$; for a sentence we write $\mathfrak A \models \sigma$.
